                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "3aafd3e2-d2d8-3b57-821c-19470e97b304",
                        "entry_id": "6bc9290a-6fd1-3939-bc19-b1549cd2bf16",
                        "glosses": [
                            "прост. неповоротливый, неуклюжий человек; увалень"
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": []
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "31e997ca-5e05-3350-9eee-ba6e1d1971f7",
                        "entry_id": "6bc9290a-6fd1-3939-bc19-b1549cd2bf16",
                        "glosses": [
                            "прост. не очень умный, медленно соображающий человек; болван"
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "4971f32a-6a00-4f92-82a2-8443d097ce21",
                                "sense_id": "31e997ca-5e05-3350-9eee-ba6e1d1971f7",
                                "text": "Бедный Василий Иванович совсем сражен и не знает, как ему быть… Решительно он какой-то пентюх с женщинами."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "03e945db-004b-47a4-b074-6fe882815b62",
                                "sense_id": "31e997ca-5e05-3350-9eee-ba6e1d1971f7",
                                "text": "Она, бедняжка, сего никогда не видала от мужа своего, деревенского пентюха, и менее нежели через час, смеясь и плача, сделалась моею."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "4010bd55-2c99-4ec1-acc6-7e5243b8a7a1",
                                "sense_id": "31e997ca-5e05-3350-9eee-ba6e1d1971f7",
                                "text": "Помилуйте, Аркадий Артемьич; мы, конечно, не то чтобы в отдалённости пробавлялись, за морем не бывали, точно; в Петербурхе больше понатерлись: всё-таки — не такие уже, однако, пентюхи, чтобы, примером будучи, коровы от свиньи не спознать."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "e218738c-645c-49e4-844a-07be2fb31b18",
                        "source_entry_id": "6bc9290a-6fd1-3939-bc19-b1549cd2bf16",
                        "target_lang_word": "clodhopper",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "clodhopper",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "ce8ecc54-fc3a-3cc3-a846-f0383414f954",
                                        "entry_id": "06f098e1-7a28-324f-b254-dbe80305a2f0",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A strong shoe for heavy-duty use, a boot."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "f748b2c5-4a18-47ca-a524-d8350f116316",
                                                "sense_id": "ce8ecc54-fc3a-3cc3-a846-f0383414f954",
                                                "text": "...who had got on his \"hill shoes,\" as he calls a pair of clodhoppers as thick as a ploughman's, and stuck round with nails."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "1882778b-d637-3b8b-8500-f92b80b4fafb",
                                        "entry_id": "06f098e1-7a28-324f-b254-dbe80305a2f0",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Any shoe construed (within a particular context) as ungainly."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "US"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "ad11c5c7-3ca6-48ff-81a4-45e93221d63a",
                                                "sense_id": "1882778b-d637-3b8b-8500-f92b80b4fafb",
                                                "text": "We had to walk slow because of his wooden clod-hoppers, and that was the way I wanted it now"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "63b760c2-3a1c-3e54-9142-8ac9ba029f7a",
                                        "entry_id": "06f098e1-7a28-324f-b254-dbe80305a2f0",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "United States Navy ankle length work shoes, distinct from dress shoes or combat boots."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "slang"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "57889715-97a0-458b-984e-a2c2f2220640",
                                                "sense_id": "63b760c2-3a1c-3e54-9142-8ac9ba029f7a",
                                                "text": "Smiling Jim Mead of New York tries on his GI clodhopper boots. He decided to return them \"because we couldn't make any altitude with those aboard.\""
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "4a822c1b-fda9-395c-a9fb-c34da9534ea2",
                                        "entry_id": "06f098e1-7a28-324f-b254-dbe80305a2f0",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A peasant or yokel."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "0def8cc9-53b5-4d1d-af8b-88173d783b1f",
                                                "sense_id": "4a822c1b-fda9-395c-a9fb-c34da9534ea2",
                                                "text": "[…] now a book is no greater rarity than bacon and greens in Virginia; and the clodhopper of this country returns from his daily labours to a book […]"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "79529075-3462-460a-982c-8ba214180b67",
                                                "sense_id": "4a822c1b-fda9-395c-a9fb-c34da9534ea2",
                                                "text": "'Nephew Jack,' he cried, looking at me when I was thinking what to say, and finding only emptiness, 'you are a heavy lout, sir; a bumpkin, a clodhopper; and I shall leave you nothing, unless it be my boots to grease.'"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "441448f6-df6e-37e5-a181-4d72821df29a",
                                        "entry_id": "06f098e1-7a28-324f-b254-dbe80305a2f0",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A clumsy or foolish person."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "UK"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "d666ca93-ea16-422e-b6f5-a7c26d9bd784",
                                                "sense_id": "441448f6-df6e-37e5-a181-4d72821df29a",
                                                "text": "All guess-work exploits shrivel up a good yard, or sometimes two, when brought to the measure, and the champion of the county dwindles into a clumsy clod-hopper."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "a86dce97-5546-3a2a-be1e-5bf462c5dfe1",
                                        "entry_id": "06f098e1-7a28-324f-b254-dbe80305a2f0",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Wheatear: any of various passerine birds."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "0bb75575-88e9-447c-8f23-71e2832dcf7b",
                                                "sense_id": "a86dce97-5546-3a2a-be1e-5bf462c5dfe1",
                                                "text": "...and as the birds then begin to resort to the downs and open commons, the \"fallow-chat,\" \"wheat-ear,\" and \"clodhopper,\" are not unappropriate names."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "957b69ab-625f-4f49-b7f2-c9f715be97b2",
                        "source_entry_id": "6bc9290a-6fd1-3939-bc19-b1549cd2bf16",
                        "target_lang_word": "bumpkin",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "bumpkin",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "29ce43f3-ff29-315b-b01a-7019b3603ac7",
                                        "entry_id": "51995d9b-b3da-3f27-bef2-811c91c4156b",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A clumsy, unsophisticated person; a yokel."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": []
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "ed332c7c-b95c-3ba9-8b8a-0e94a3e098e8",
                                        "entry_id": "51995d9b-b3da-3f27-bef2-811c91c4156b",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A short boom or spar used to extend a sail or secure a stay."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": []
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "1ae77b79-a5fd-3352-8187-129f08be7be5",
                                        "entry_id": "51995d9b-b3da-3f27-bef2-811c91c4156b",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Dance, a series of reels, Scottish."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "f0e7008a-656d-48ee-839a-ec6bbfc96f2d",
                                                "sense_id": "1ae77b79-a5fd-3352-8187-129f08be7be5",
                                                "text": "They mix with Dancers, who now advance to the front, where a bumpkin, or dance of many interwoven reels, is performed; after which the Bride is led to a seat, and some of her Maidens sit by her."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
